            <ArticleTitle>[Two-year results after implantation of a metallic stent EPHESOS in patients with coronary heart disease].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>49-52</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate two-year results of using a metallic stent EPHESOS in patients with native atherosclerotic lesion of coronary arteries (without prior interventions).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIAL AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The stent was implanted to 731 patients. 53% had manifestations of unstable angina. 42% of the lesions were complicated. The length of the stenosis was 15.5 +/- 6.8 mm, 44% stenoses were long.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The success of stenting was 99.6%. Cases of acute and subacute thrombosis were absent. Non-Q wave myocardial infarction in primary hospitalization developed in 4 patients. For 6 months, cardiovascular complications (death, angina, restenosis, repeated revascularization) occurred in 21%. Angiographic control follow-up of 6 months maximum covered 329 (45%) patients. Hemodynamically significant restenosis was detected in 18% patients. Cardiovascular complications for 24 months occurred in 39%.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The EPHESOS stent has demonstrated a stable long-term effect on prevention of thrombosis and restenosis in the majority of patients with a relatively high risk of intervention.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
